  "analysis": [
    {
      "label": "Context",
      "text": "France led 3–1 with twenty-five minutes left. Croatia had played extra time in three straight knockout rounds and were pushing bodies forward, which left the space in front of their box unguarded."
    },
    {
      "label": "Build-up",
      "text": "France simply kept the ball — Matuidi, Pogba, Nzonzi, back to Pogba — until Croatia's midfield had been pulled across and Hernández could carry it forward on the left."
    },
    {
      "label": "The strike",
      "text": "The ball came to Mbappé about twenty-five metres out with nobody closing. There was no touch to set it and almost no backlift: a low drive struck as the ball arrived."
    },
    {
      "label": "Why it worked",
      "text": "Subašić was screened by the Croatian defenders and had no wind-up to read. Low and across the keeper is the hardest ball to reach from that range."
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "label": "Shoot from the edge",
      "text": "When a tiring defence drops off, the space in front of the box is free. A shot from there beats trying to play through a packed area."
    },
    {
      "label": "No backlift",
      "text": "Striking without a wind-up gives the keeper nothing to react to. Hit through the ball with the body over it rather than leaning back."
    },
    {
      "label": "Keep it low",
      "text": "A low shot forces the keeper all the way to the ground and can be hidden by defenders' legs until it is too late."
    },
    {
      "label": "Make them chase",
      "text": "France's patient passing before the goal was not caution; it was what pulled Croatia out of shape and created the opening."
    }
  ],
